  Status: source-grounded design, not a verification certificate. Final UV
  Chapters 4\U12 have a combined budget of <strong|220 pages>: 18 + 26 + 24 +
  26 + 18 + 30 + 24 + 30 + 24. The stable legacy IDs below are retained for
  dependencies: U1 is now part of foundation Chapter 2; U2\UU5 are Chapters
  4\U7; U6 is split into Chapters 8\U9; U7\UU9 are Chapters 10\U12. Free
  Maxwell comparison C1 is Chapter 3. All paths below are relative to the
  project root. Source labels are authoritative locators; line numbers refer
  to the supplied snapshot.

  <section|Instructions common to every UV
  author><label|instructions-common-to-every-uv-author>

  Read this file's conventions, your chapter specification, its stated
  inputs, and only the indicated source passages first. Read
  <verbatim|uv-audit.md> before turning any conditional estimate into a
  theorem. The notation glossary and master blueprint prevail over
  provisional symbols below. Do not inherit a source's assertion that a
  previous package \Psupplies\Q a hypothesis without checking that the same
  coupling, path, masks, Gaussian law, normalization, support norm, and
  finite family occur on both sides.

  Use nine independent writing units, but do not pretend their mathematical
  dependencies are independent. In particular, first prove
  <strong|conditional one-step analytic estimates for an arbitrary admissible
  adjacent pair>; choose a simultaneous infinite schedule only once the
  finite-stage requirements have been collected. Existence of a schedule
  never proves an analytic estimate. Similarly, retain finite-cutoff Maxwell
  comparison in U8, and move limit rank and stress assertions of Outline
  III.11 to the continuum chapters.

  The local section chapter U1 and free Maxwell chapter C1 may be written
  early. U2\UU6 produce the unmarked transformation; U7 produces a common
  trajectory. U8 uses U1\UU7 and C1. U9 uses U8. Individual subordinate
  propositions may be stated conditionally before their hypotheses are
  discharged, with that conditional status explicit in their titles. No
  global existence theorem may be stated as proved until those hypotheses
  have been discharged on the chosen family.

  Cite existing arguments rather than reprove them. A citation to a method is
  not a theorem with the exact required hypotheses. Write the short
  normalization/admissibility check when applying a known theorem. New work
  here is often the uniformity, large-field complement, and compatibility
  with the chosen density, not the elementary analytic argument. The author's
  working notes should distinguish a checked application, a proposed
  adaptation, and an unverified source claim.

  At every first appearance define a technical symbol in prose and an
  equation. When an unusual symbol has not appeared for roughly three pages,
  add a one-clause reminder or a definition reference. Avoid \Ppaid,\Q
  \Pfunded,\Q \Preceipt,\Q \Pcoin,\Q \Preadout,\Q \Phealthy,\Q \Pwarning,\Q
  \Pcompiler,\Q \Pport,\Q \Pcourse,\Q and \Phorizon\Q as monograph
  terminology. Their source meanings are: quantified bound, admissibility
  estimate, coefficient vector, Taylor projection, small-field support,
  Gaussian tail event, connected expansion, input/output condition, auxiliary
  coupling sequence, and collection of Gaussian kernels. Some substitutions
  depend on context; do not perform a global search-and-replace.

  <section|Shared conventions for these chapters><label|shared-conventions-for-these-chapters>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Fix the compact matrix group <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>>,
    probability Haar measure, and Lie algebra metric
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>>=-n<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Retr><around|(|X*Y|)>>.
    The adjoint Casimir in this metric is <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>>;
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>=11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>.
    Reserve (b\<gtr\>0) for the block-kernel penalty, never for a norm
    record.

    <item>The physical torus side is <math|R<rsub|p>>, replacing
    Scott/Trish's <math|L<rsub|p>>. Set <math|D<rsub|0>=1>,
    <math|D<rsub|m+1>=L<rsub|m>*D<rsub|m>>,
    <math|a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>, <math|\<tau\><rsub|m>=log
    D<rsub|m>>. <math|L<rsub|m>> is an odd <strong|level-dependent> block
    ratio. The original files usually write <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> for this
    ratio. Blocking is <math|m+1\<to\>m>; <math|N> is the ultraviolet cutoff.
    The lattice has <math|R<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> sites in each direction. Do
    not assume constant block ratios.

    <item><math|J<rsub|m>> is the nondecreasing stage index, tending to
    infinity, with steps at most one. The stage and level indices must never
    be silently interchanged.

    <item><math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>>
    is the reference inverse coupling. <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>> is the
    actual one. Their band is <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>=<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|]>>.
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> is the common level-zero value.

    <item>For the Scott formulation, <math|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>=exp
    <around|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)>>,
    <math|t<rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>*M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>>,
    <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>,
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>>,
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|lf>>=exp
    <around|(|-<around|(|M<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|cut>>|)><rsup|1/5>|)>>.
    These are definitions of that formulation, not automatically the
    numerical functions in every Martin argument. A translation check is
    required before importing estimates involving <verbatim|hot>,
    <verbatim|fineCut>, or <verbatim|fieldCut> from Martin.

    <item><math|V<rsub|d>> is the space of invariant radial-gauge jet
    polynomials of scaling dimension at most <math|d>. A nested real basis
    <math|p<rsub|i>> has weight <math|w<rsub|i>> and factor number
    <math|f<rsub|i>>. The diagonal map <math|S<rsub|t>> multiplies coordinate
    <math|i> by <math|t<rsup|f<rsub|i>>>; it is distinct from spatial
    scaling, which multiplies a weight-<math|w> polynomial by
    <math|h<rsup|w>>. <math|c<rsup|\<circ\>>=c-c<rsub|0>*e<rsub|0>>.

    <item><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> denotes the fixed finite lattice stencil;
    <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|c|]>> is its local
    polynomial representative. (r(d)) is the reference level.
    <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> is the coefficient transfer map to that
    level, not the partition function <math|Z<rsub|N,p>>.

    <item><math|E<rsub|i>> are small-field local activities anchored at
    <math|o<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>>; <math|K<rsub|i>> are large-field polymer
    activities on <math|Y<rsub|i>> with nonempty flagged inventory
    <math|I<rsub|i>>. Activities need not be positive. Their sum gives the
    actual positive density on a constructed trajectory.

    <item><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\>>> is the integral block map.
    <math|H<rsub|N,p,m><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c|)>> is an inserted,
    unnormalized density, and <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,x|)>> is the
    normalized physical insertion. Do not divide by <math|K<rsub|i>> when
    defining marked activities; they can vanish.

    <item>All geometric distances in lattice estimates are periodic
    maximum-coordinate distances, unless explicitly converted to Euclidean
    distance. Physical distance includes a factor <math|a<rsub|m>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Foundation Chapter 2 (U1): local gauge-invariant observables \V
  integrated with foundations><label|foundation-chapter-2-u1-local-gauge-invariant-observables--integrated-with-foundations>

  <strong|Purpose and input.> Starting only from the lattice/Wilson chapter
  and elementary differential geometry of <math|G>, define the
  finite-dimensional label spaces and realize them by finite-stencil
  observables. The construction does not presuppose continuum fields or their
  null spaces.

  <strong|Sections, in order.> (1) Radial gauge and curvature jets, including
  the relation between homogeneous potential and curvature jets. (2)
  Engineering weight, polynomial degree/factor number, invariant spaces
  <math|V<rsub|d>>, conjugation and orthogonal action. (3) Holonomies of
  truncated potentials and the scaled Taylor map. (4) A finite plaquette
  sampler, equivariant polynomial inverse to the necessary jet order, and
  finite stencils. (5) Small-field derivative bounds. (6) Equation
  observables as a separate exact integration-by-parts construction;
  distinguish the free equation ideal from the as-yet-unconstructed continuum
  null space. End with the curvature-square example.

  <strong|Required output.> For every <math|d>, construct a real,
  symmetry-equivariant linear map <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>C<around|(|G<rsup|E<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|)>>|)>>,
  gauge invariant and with <math|P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|1|]>=1>,
  whose evaluation on scaled radial holonomies has the prescribed Taylor
  polynomial through order <math|d>. Each pure-factor column admits a local
  derivative bound <math|C<rsub|d,k,P>*t<rsup|f<rsub|i>>>, uniformly over
  periods admitting the stencil, for each fixed derivative order and spatial
  weight. State separately the dressed equation-section assertion: for each
  fixed degree there is an equivariant invertible change of label coordinates
  such that an equation insertion has zero Wilson expectation against every
  bounded measurable spectator depending only on links outside a fixed
  neighborhood, for every sufficiently large lattice period and every nonzero
  inverse coupling. Its application to limiting null spaces is deferred.

  <strong|Source packet.> Scott Outline II.1, propositions II.1.1\UII.1.5,
  and III.16.8\UIII.16.10 for the algebra. Scott
  <verbatim|part2/A-section.tex>: <verbatim|sec:A-labels> (9),
  <verbatim|lem:A1> (85), <verbatim|lem:A-bounds> (142), <verbatim|lem:A-R4>
  (181), <verbatim|sec:A-example> (255). Martin
  <verbatim|existence/local-sections.tex>, <verbatim|sec:local-sections>,
  especially finite sampler (8), polynomial recovery (71), averaging (117),
  bounds (157); <verbatim|local-splitting.tex> and
  <verbatim|actual-equation-kernel-relay.tex> are follow-up sources for the
  dressed equation construction. Trish's A file is nearly identical, so
  compare only changed statements.

  <strong|Checks and compression.> Cite matrix ODE existence,
  finite-group/Reynolds averaging and finite-dimensional inverse-function
  arguments; give the actual sampler and why its differential is injective.
  Do not identify \Pon-shell polynomial\Q with a physical null field yet. The
  stencil is finite but need not be explicit in coordinates. Export
  <math|V<rsub|d>,\<Sigma\><rsub|d>,P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>>,S<rsub|t>>,
  the equation ideal <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|d>>, and the full quantified local
  derivative estimates to U8 and C1.

  <section|Chapter 4 (U2). Exact blocking and effective densities \V 18
  pages><label|chapter-4-u2-exact-blocking-and-effective-densities--18-pages>

  <strong|Input.> Wilson measure and lattice geometry; U1 only for notation
  used in examples.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Center-normalized local gauges and axial gauges. (2)
  Averaged parallel transports and the nearest-group-point predictor. (3)
  Extension to every configuration by a finite random choice of
  frames/connectors; normalized probability kernel. (4) Small/large plaquette
  fields and admissible local activities. (5) Exact covering sum, local
  derivative seminorm and weighted polymer norms. (6) A provisional
  admissibility class for one adjacent pair, with all constants treated as
  inputs.

  <strong|Required output.> Define <math|k<rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>\<geqslant\>0>,
  prove <math|<big|int>k<rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=1>,
  gauge covariance and lattice symmetry, and exact preservation of partition
  integrals. The density representation is

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><around|(|U|)>=exp <around|[|-A<rsub|m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>1<rsub|X<rsub|i><text|
    small>>*E<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>+v<rsub|m><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|m>|\|>|]>*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\>>K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>,
  </equation*>

  where selected supports are disjoint and their inventories cover all
  flagged sites exactly. Define locality on the <strong|full read support>,
  including flags and extra eligibility tests. Distinguish anchored regular
  sums from sums over all polymers containing a site. Define Borel masks and
  derivatives with masks fixed; the clipped action is not globally smooth.
  Leave actual path coefficients as parameters until U5.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline II.2 and III.1; Scott
  <verbatim|part1/05-renormalization.tex>, <verbatim|def:D04> (41),
  <verbatim|def:D06> (105), <verbatim|def:D05a> (136), <verbatim|def:D05b>
  (148), and equations <verbatim|eq:I12-clipping>,
  <verbatim|eq:I12-path-action>; <verbatim|part2/B-section.tex>,
  <verbatim|subsec:B-kernel> (19) through <verbatim|lem:B1> (119);
  <verbatim|part2/D-section.tex>, definitions D24\UD25a (21\U67). Martin
  <verbatim|lattice.tex>, <verbatim|haar-chart.tex>,
  <verbatim|local-mean.tex>, <verbatim|predictor-branch.tex> supply expanded
  local geometry; read their named subsections before importing a branch
  argument.

  <strong|Checks.> The density has every branch and the empty covering
  convention; it is not merely its small-field approximation. Standard
  Haar/Fubini arguments receive a short citation/application, not lengthy
  proofs. Do not call all density representations positive without proving
  that they represent an actual transformed Wilson density.

  <section|Chapter 5 (U3). Gaussian kernels and stationary backgrounds \V 26
  pages><label|chapter-5-u3-gaussian-kernels-and-stationary-backgrounds--26-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U2 block map, local gauge slices, norms and conditional
  adjacent-level parameters. Ordinary finite-dimensional Gaussian integration
  and Fourier theory are background knowledge.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Lattice differential forms, zero modes and the gauge
  slice. (2) Linearized block averaging, Fourier aliases and the Schur
  complement. (3) Exponentially weighted kernels, strip analyticity and
  positive row factorizations. (4) A volume-independent vertical Hessian gap.
  (5) Analytic stationary background and a uniform coordinate ball. (6)
  Gauge-tree changes, Haar Jacobian and determinant compatibility. (7)
  Localized inverse and vertex derivatives.

  <strong|Required output.> For admissible incoming/outgoing Gaussian data
  and each allowed period, prove the exact quadratic minimization and
  covariance identities on the specified gauge slice; retain
  harmonic/zero-momentum contributions explicitly. Obtain a positive lower
  Hessian bound and exponentially weighted inverse bounds uniform in the
  period, hence a stationary branch with a common analytic radius. State
  precisely which estimates are pre-<math|L>, post-<math|L>, or
  large-coupling estimates. The curvature covariance identity is
  <math|C<rsub|m+1>-W<rsub|m>*C<rsub|m>*W<rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>=d<rsub|f>*\<Gamma\><rsub|m>*d<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>>,
  with <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|m>\<succeq\>0>, under the full
  compatible-quadratic-data assumptions. Prove tree independence only for the
  combined stationary value/Haar/determinant quantity, with the Jacobian
  factors shown.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline III.3, III.6, III.16.5\UIII.16.7; Scott B
  <verbatim|lem:B2> (242), <verbatim|lem:B3> (409), <verbatim|lem:B4> (501),
  <verbatim|lem:B7> (584), <verbatim|lem:B5> (665). Martin
  <verbatim|free-kernel-components.tex>, <verbatim|free-kernel-estimates.tex>,
  <verbatim|weighted-stationary-inverses.tex>, and
  <verbatim|uniform-stationary-chart.tex>: local derivative/Hessian data (8),
  common-ball contraction (73), derivatives (138). For cutoff kernel
  convergence later, retain <verbatim|flat-step.tex>,
  <verbatim|prop:ex-lattice-flat-step> (375), and
  <verbatim|flat-estimate.tex>, <verbatim|prop:ex-lattice-flat-rate> (12),
  but do not prove that separate recursion here.

  <strong|Literature and checks.> Baªaban's propagator/background-field
  papers are the original method, but neither the precise soft kernel nor the
  period-uniform hypotheses can be inferred from a citation alone. Replace
  routine inverse differentiation by its formula and reference. Do not use
  the one-loop matching to prove existence of the stationary branch whose
  determinant defines that matching. Split fresh matching assumptions from
  the analytic branch theorem.

  <section|Chapter 6 (U4). Large fields and local Gaussian integration \V 24
  pages><label|chapter-6-u4-large-fields-and-local-gaussian-integration--24-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U2 exact sectors and U3 positive quadratic data/stationary
  branch. Work on one arbitrary admissible adjacent pair.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Defect sets, connected neighborhoods and bounded
  overlap. (2) Completing squares and allocating positive energy. (3) Fine
  plaquette, predictor mismatch and coarse plaquette bounds. (4) Exact
  changes from Haar variables to Gaussian coordinates. (5) Localized
  covariance, relative determinants and scalar normalization. (6)
  Simultaneous Gaussian tail events and uniform derivative bounds.

  <strong|Required output.> Establish a deterministic lower bound with a
  strictly positive energy per independent defect, sufficient to dominate
  entropy and all later polynomial losses. Prove the exact local integral
  formula with its chart indicators, tags, Jacobians, frozen links, affine
  shifts, Gaussian density and positive scalar. State a simultaneous tail
  estimate indexed by <strong|distinct tail-event addresses>; marginal tail
  estimates multiplied as if independent are not sufficient. Prove
  determinant/inverse estimates on full read supports and on the actual
  covariance used in the integral, not on an unrelated flat Gaussian model.
  The output is a common integrable majorant for each fixed derivative order,
  uniform in periods, fields, allowed sector data and coupling interval.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline III.4 and III.10 steps 1\U10; Scott B
  <verbatim|subsec:B-energy> (761), <verbatim|lem:B-D1> (842),
  <verbatim|subsec:B-sector> (964), <verbatim|lem:B-windows> (1009),
  <verbatim|lem:B-D2> (1157). Martin <verbatim|seed-energy.tex> (especially
  189\U301), <verbatim|seed-hit-comparison.tex>,
  <verbatim|seed-unhit-comparison.tex>, <verbatim|gaussian-moment-prices.tex>,
  <verbatim|gaussian-warnings.tex>, <verbatim|warning-safety.tex>,
  <verbatim|actual-palette-warning.tex>, <verbatim|actual-harvested-owner-energy.tex>,
  and <verbatim|actual-local-compiler-handoff.tex>: matrix/support estimates
  (52), determinant derivatives (112), interval uniformity (225),
  <verbatim|lem:actual-neutral-warning> (257).

  <strong|Checks.> Keep energy loss, determinant normalization and Gaussian
  tail costs distinct until their common bound is proved. Martin's last two
  actual-data files are candidate missing compatibility suppliers, not
  interchangeable estimates. Mark their assumptions explicitly. Cite
  Combes\UThomas decay and standard Gaussian moment formulas; derive only
  their required uniform application. See audit A4/A10.

  <section|Chapter 7 (U5). Local matching and the running coupling
  coefficient \V 26 pages><label|chapter-7-u5-local-matching-and-the-running-coupling-coefficient--26-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U3 stationary action/determinants, U4 local bounds, U1
  invariant jets. No all-scale trajectory yet.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Translation kernels and gauge Ward identities for
  Taylor coefficients. (2) Quantitative division through lattice curl. (3)
  Cubic/quartic classical matching and the fixed path action. (4) Gauge
  completion of the one-loop determinant, including Haar and ghost
  contributions. (5) One-loop annulus calculation and normalization. (6)
  Extraction of the unique invariant quadratic coefficient and estimates for
  the remaining quadratic kernel. (7) Stability under one downward step and
  stronger exponential-weight reserve.

  <strong|Required output.> Construct path coefficients with the stated
  symmetry, weighted bounds and no residual curvature-square coefficient in
  the loop correction. Prove the quantum quadratic coefficient is
  <math|-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L+O<rsub|j><around|(|1|)>>, the error uniform
  in periods and admissible inputs, with <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>=11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>
  in the fixed trace metric. Distinguish this coefficient theorem from the
  exact nonlinear coupling-reset theorem of U6. Record the
  derivative/parameter dependence required later, not only a value bound.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline III.7 and the fresh-data portion of III.6;
  Scott C <verbatim|lem:C1> (44), <verbatim|lem:C2> (62), <verbatim|lem:C3>
  (87), <verbatim|lem:C4> (147), <verbatim|lem:C5> (185), <verbatim|lem:C6>
  (211), <verbatim|lem:C7> (266). Martin <verbatim|stationary-output-construction.tex>:
  Ward data (10), leading coefficient (68), cubic/quartic matching (109),
  quadratic extraction (190), loop remainder (277),
  <verbatim|prop:stationary-output-path> (359);
  <verbatim|multileg-curvature-lift.tex>,
  <verbatim|quadratic-lattice-symmetry.tex>,
  <verbatim|thermal-contact-cancellation.tex>,
  <verbatim|free-collision-pole-separation.tex>, and
  <verbatim|free-maxwell-radial-flux.tex> give supplementary detailed routes.

  <strong|Literature and checks.> Cite Gross\UWilczek and Politzer for the
  one-loop coefficient and Abbott for background-field calculation; prove the
  bounded difference between that calculation and this exact lattice block
  map. A perturbative coefficient citation alone does not establish that
  comparison. Do not discard contact terms before explaining why they cannot
  change the extracted coefficient. If a quantitative curl-division lemma is
  already available in the needed weighted norm, cite it; otherwise prove
  that adaptation concisely.

  <section|Chapter 8 (U6a). Abstract polymer and connected expansions \V 18
  pages><label|chapter-8-u6a-abstract-polymer-and-connected-expansions--18-pages>

  <strong|Input.> Chapter 4's supports, anchored regular activities,
  containing-site large-field norms and exact-cover conventions. The abstract
  result needs only a countable incompatibility relation and complex
  activities, so it can be written independently of the nonlinear gauge
  estimates.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Compatible gas and connected coefficients, including
  repeated labels and factorials. (2) The Kotecký\UPreiss convergence
  criterion, stated with a precise rooted bound and cited rather than
  reproved. (3) Weighted supports and conversion from anchored to
  containing-site sums. (4) Several squarefree sources and bounds for
  connected marked coefficients. (5) Exact-cover large-field sums:
  distinguish the algebraic reorganization needed here from the unconstrained
  gas theorem. (6) Locality, parameter continuity under common domination,
  and the interface to Chapter 9.

  <strong|Required output.> State a standard absolute-convergence theorem
  with its actual incompatibility-sum hypothesis, and prove only the short
  reductions needed to apply it to the chosen support weights and finite
  source algebra. Show explicitly which source-specific bounds remain inputs:
  energy suppression, integrated Gaussian derivative bounds, eligibility
  events and exact cover reconstruction. No abstract polymer theorem
  establishes these estimates. A rooted estimate must charge all supports
  containing the root, not just activities anchored there. Count a repeated
  activity label correctly in the connected series even though compatible
  configurations contain each selected polymer once.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline II.2, III.5, III.8 connected coefficients,
  III.15. Scott D <verbatim|lem:D-rooted> (111), <verbatim|lem:D-connected>
  (128), <verbatim|lem:D-fusion> (546). Martin
  <verbatim|cluster-expansion.tex>: cancellation (41), tree bound (56), root
  anywhere (132), exact logarithm (187). <verbatim|high-degree-cluster-prices.tex>,
  <verbatim|soft-log-fusion.tex>, and <verbatim|forced-exclusion.tex> are
  follow-up sources for marked and exact-cover applications. The parent
  editor verified Ueltschi 2004, <hlink|full
  text|https://arxiv.org/html/math-ph/0304003v3>, Theorem 1 and equations
  (3)\U(4), as a convenient precise formulation of the standard criterion and
  rooted bound; Theorems 2\U3 cover correlations. Cite Kotecký\UPreiss for
  origin and Ueltschi for the form used.

  <strong|Boundary.> This chapter exports the abstract tool and norm
  conversions. The exact Gaussian integral, the concrete activities, and the
  proof that they obey its hypotheses belong to Chapter 9 and preceding
  analytic chapters. Do not reproduce Chapter 9's nonlinear construction
  here.

  <section|Chapter 9 (U6b). The nonlinear RG step \V 30
  pages><label|chapter-9-u6b-the-nonlinear-rg-step--30-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U2 density class, U3\UU5 conditional estimates. Use Chapter
  8 for the abstract polymer criterion and prove its model-specific
  hypotheses here.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Apply Chapter 8 to the concrete local Gaussian
  expansion. (2) Integrated activity derivatives and spatial support weights.
  (3) Inherited terms, auxiliary-period localization and local quadratic
  subtraction. (4) Large-field polymer regrouping and exact coverage. (5)
  Reassembly of the genuine block integral. (6) Scalar coupling reset and
  parameter continuity. (7) Wilson initialization. (8) The complete one-step
  theorem, with its finite list of stage requirements.

  <strong|Required output (RG).> For every <math|N,p,m\<less\>N>, every
  compact real parameter interval, and every continuous admissible fine
  family <math|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>> with couplings
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>, construct a
  continuous admissible coarse family <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>> such that
  pointwise

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|u>=\<cal-B\><rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>>*\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>,<space|2em>\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|u>|)>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|m>+e<rsub|u>,<space|1em><around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>.
  </equation*>

  The error bound is independent of <math|N,p,u> and the chosen curve. The
  output regular and large-field norms obey the next-scale allowances. The
  construction must be fixed on entire curves with restriction compatibility;
  selecting unrelated output representations point by point is insufficient
  for U7 shooting and convergence. Record the stronger weighted
  analytic/continuity outputs needed for U7 separately.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline III.5, III.8 (connected coefficients only),
  III.9, III.10 steps 8\U12, III.15; Scott D: <verbatim|lem:D-rooted> (111),
  <verbatim|lem:D-connected> (128), <verbatim|lem:D-inherited> (254),
  <verbatim|lem:D-localization> (366), <verbatim|lem:D-local-subtraction>
  (490), <verbatim|lem:D-fusion> (546), <verbatim|lem:D1> (574),
  <verbatim|lem:D5> (603), <verbatim|lem:C8> (663), <verbatim|lem:D6> (732),
  <verbatim|lem:D3> (772), <verbatim|lem:D2> (843). Martin
  <verbatim|cluster-expansion.tex>: repeated labels, weighted tree estimate
  (56), exact logarithm (187); <verbatim|gaussian-reassembly.tex>: exact
  coordinate factors (13), restoring masks (87), absolute expansion (201),
  connected factorization (256); <verbatim|coupling-reset-selection.tex>:
  separating field (10), scalar equation (53), identification with local
  coefficient (126); <verbatim|actual-corrected-body-compiler.tex>,
  especially genuine density (270) and corrected forced output (416), for
  compatibility-sensitive applications.

  <strong|Literature and checks.> State and cite Kotecký\UPreiss rather than
  reproduce an abstract cluster-expansion proof; show the activities satisfy
  its criterion and how source norms are incorporated. If using the
  repeated-label connected series, spell out the factorial convention and
  choose a matching formulation. No division by a vanishing polymer.
  Pointwise equality of the full integral, continuity, locality and both
  outgoing norms are separate conclusions. The one-loop approximation is not
  an approximation to this identity.

  <section|Chapter 10 (U7). A common trajectory and fixed-scale stability \V
  24 pages><label|chapter-10-u7-a-common-trajectory-and-fixed-scale-stability--24-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U6 one-step theorem including continuation/continuity and
  stronger norm reserves, U5 path stability. U8's future finite-degree
  requests may be listed as explicit numerical requirements, but their
  analytic validity must already be established in conditional form before
  closing the simultaneous construction.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Order of constants and finite-stage requests. (2)
  Increasing block ratios, stage plateaux, periods and reference scales. (3)
  Wilson shooting to the common endpoint. (4) Uniform logarithmic running.
  (5) Cutoff comparison of quadratic kernels, then cubic/quartic and loop
  coefficients. (6) Common activity dictionaries and fixed-scale convergence.
  (7) Retaining one family and all its transition witnesses.

  <strong|Required output (MT).> One schedule and one collection of
  trajectories for all <math|N,p>, before a requested volume subsequence,
  with Wilson start, endpoint <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,0>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>,
  exact block identities and all admissible bands. For every
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, some <math|m<rsub|0>>, independent of
  <math|N,p>, satisfies

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*\<tau\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*\<tau\><rsub|m>*<space|1em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed <math|m,p>, couplings and activities converge in one specified
  weighted space as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>; <strong|no convergence of
  accumulated scalar normalizations is asserted>. Use normalized density
  ratios later. Provide a source-backed adjacent-step stability theorem on a
  fixed input; graph contraction alone cannot give stability of maps that
  themselves vary with the cutoff.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline III.2, III.9 shooting, III.14; Scott
  <verbatim|part1/09-constants.tex>, all rows 1\U11; E <verbatim|lem:E1.1>
  (112), <verbatim|lem:E1.2> (160), <verbatim|lem:E1.3> (199); D
  <verbatim|lem:D-cutoff-continuity> (1069), <verbatim|lem:D-E1> (1122),
  <verbatim|lem:D-fixed-level> (1164). Martin
  <verbatim|common-funded-history.tex>, <verbatim|common-scale-selection.tex>,
  <verbatim|surviving-interval-shooting.tex>, <verbatim|running-coupling.tex>
  (<verbatim|prop:running-asymptotic>, 105); <verbatim|flat-step.tex> and
  <verbatim|flat-estimate.tex>; <verbatim|selected-history-limits.tex>,
  especially auxiliary service (147), activity/coupling limits (317),
  <verbatim|lem:selected-chartlet-matching> (378);
  <verbatim|public-run-binding.tex> and <verbatim|original-history-portfolio-attachment.tex>
  for common-family identity.

  <strong|Checks.> The Outline III.13 coordinated construction is an
  alternative, not a replacement for this family. Its convergence results may
  be used only after proving equality of families or reproducing its
  hypotheses for this one. An infinite-dimensional norm ball is not compact.
  Use finite truncation plus uniform contraction/tails. The scalar drift must
  be divided by <math|log L<rsub|m>> before summing; a stage error
  <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|1|)>> alone is not summable. Include the finitely
  many initial indices rather than hiding them in \Peventually.\Q

  <section|Chapter 11 (U8). Insertions, Gaussian comparison and uniform
  difference bounds \V 30 pages><label|chapter-11-u8-insertions-gaussian-comparison-and-uniform-difference-bounds--30-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U1 sections/equation observables; U2\UU7; C1 free Maxwell
  quotient, strict reflected Gram positivity and finite separating detectors.
  Treat C1 as a mathematical predecessor, not a forward reference to the
  final continuum comparison theorem.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) One inserted density, primary coefficient,
  vanishing-jet regular remainder and large-field remainder. (2)
  Reference-level normalization and the physical unit. (3) Several insertions
  using squarefree source variables, including joint large-field terminal
  modes. (4) Replicated sources and centered chessboard bounds. (5) Exact
  auxiliary restart for comparison and its full Gaussian law. (6) Corrected
  finite-cutoff free comparison, projections and finite detectors. (7)
  Equation errors, local matching and normalized lattice differences. (8)
  Separated correlation bounds uniform in the volume.

  <strong|Required outputs.> Construct an invertible
  <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>>, respecting reality/symmetry, and

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|N,p><around|(|d,c;x|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*P<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|loc>><around|[|S<rsub|1/t<rsub|N>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The unit coefficient is <math|a<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>><rsup|d>*e<rsub|0>>,
  because the RG rescaling uses degree <math|d> of the whole space, not the
  individual basis weight. The exact inserted recursion is
  <math|H<rsub|m>=L<rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<cal-B\><rsub|m><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|m+1>>*H<rsub|m+1>>.
  State coefficient/remainder estimates on this same density. For every
  finite tuple <math|v>, finite slot-difference list <math|l>, and
  (r\<gtr\>0), there are <math|C,N<rsub|0>>, independent of <math|p>, such
  that $ \|\\Delta_l M_{N,p}(v;y)\|\\le C$ for all
  <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all tuples with periodic physical
  separation at least <math|r>. Every normalized difference carries
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>; a bound for undifferenced fields does not imply
  this one.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline II.1.6\UII.1.10, III.8 locality, III.11
  finite-cutoff estimates only, III.12.1\UIII.12.5, III.15. Scott E
  <verbatim|def:D26> (230), <verbatim|lem:E2.1> (255), <verbatim|def:D27>
  (314), <verbatim|lem:E2.3> (354), <verbatim|lem:E2.6> (417),
  <verbatim|lem:E2.7> (438), <verbatim|lem:E2.8> (456), <verbatim|lem:E2.9>
  (474), <verbatim|lem:E2-free-tests> (498), <verbatim|lem:E2.10> (544),
  <verbatim|lem:E2.4> (618); companions <verbatim|E-one-mark.tex>,
  <verbatim|E-several-mark.tex>, <verbatim|E-Gaussian-sources.tex>,
  <verbatim|E-local-comparison.tex>, <verbatim|E-bounded-real.tex>. Read
  <strong|Trish> <verbatim|E-several-mark.tex>,
  <verbatim|lem:E-joint-terminal> (52), for joint terminal modes and
  <verbatim|E-Gaussian-sources.tex>, <verbatim|lem:E-course-mass> (14), for
  exact mass preservation under a different comparison sequence. Its
  conditional paragraph after <verbatim|lem:E-source-polynomial> must be
  explicitly addressed.

  <strong|Detailed candidate repair packet.> Martin
  <verbatim|actual-marked-portfolio-calibration.tex>,
  <verbatim|original-history-portfolio-attachment.tex>;
  <verbatim|actual-local-compiler-handoff.tex> (327\U404);
  <verbatim|restarted-rank-history.tex>, <verbatim|prop:restart-endpoint>
  (243), <verbatim|prop:restart-actual-closure> (564, proof 581\U742);
  <verbatim|actual-mixed-marked-compiler.tex>;
  <verbatim|allarity-planted-compiler.tex>,
  <verbatim|all-arity-reader-gaussian-model.tex>,
  <verbatim|actual-reader-local-gaussian-prices.tex>,
  <verbatim|actual-reader-guarded-curvature.tex>,
  <verbatim|actual-reader-tree-covariance.tex>,
  <verbatim|actual-reader-short-covariance.tex>,
  <verbatim|actual-reader-small-window-bridge.tex>;
  <verbatim|fullmesh-fixedbounds-producer.tex>,
  <verbatim|lem:ex-fullmesh-cap> (31), <verbatim|prop:ex-fullmesh-cap-source>
  (68), full difference theorem (144), <verbatim|prop:fullmesh-selected-supplier>
  (496). These detailed sections require a hypothesis-by-hypothesis
  comparison; no blanket claim that all Trish caveats are resolved is
  authorized by this blueprint.

  <strong|Checks and compression.> Squarefree source algebra is standard;
  show only the exact density correspondence and new large-field joint modes.
  The centered moment bound is a volume-uniform estimate of a replicated
  integral. In the finite-detector argument bound the projected coefficient
  and absorb half its norm; do not assume bounded raw differenced
  coefficients. Comparisons on an auxiliary restart preserve original
  normalized integrals, not equality of the two lower-level densities. Do not
  deduce continuum rank here.

  <section|Chapter 12 (U9). The full ultraviolet limit at fixed volume \V 24
  pages><label|chapter-12-u9-the-full-ultraviolet-limit-at-fixed-volume--24-pages>

  <strong|Input.> U7 fixed-scale stability on the selected family and U8
  actual inserted densities, replicated-source error bounds and all finite
  differences. No infinite-volume extraction is required to prove the
  principal theorem.

  <strong|Sections.> (1) Common intermediate level and scalar-normalized
  densities. (2) Contraction of the complete marked remainder graph and
  convergence of coefficient maps. (3) Joint marked expression, removal of
  higher modes and stopping error. (4) Cauchy convergence on common lattice
  points. (5) Equicontinuity, all position derivatives and interpolation. (6)
  Separated smearing and statement of the exact ultraviolet interface.
  Optional short final section: analytic endpoint dependence, only if needed
  downstream.

  <strong|Required output (UV).> For every fixed (p,n,v), obtain a smooth
  periodic <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>> on
  <math|<around|{|x:x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>\<nin\>R<rsub|p>*\<bbb-Z\><rsup|4>,i\<ne\>j|}>>.
  For every finite difference list <math|l> and smooth test compactly
  supported away from periodic collisions inside an admissible period cell,

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>> a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f*<around|(|a<rsub|N>*y|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|l>*M<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*\<partial\><rsub|l>*M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation*>

  The inner limit is the whole cutoff sequence. Explain separately that
  thresholds for fixed-volume convergence can depend on <math|p>, whereas
  U8's compact separated bounds are uniform in <math|p>. Coincident
  coordinate projections are allowed when points themselves are distinct.
  Export finite reflection positivity and the volume-uniform derivative
  bounds to C2.

  <strong|Source packet.> Outline III.12.6\UIII.12.8; Scott E
  <verbatim|sec:partII-E.3> (663), <verbatim|lem:E3.1> (703),
  <verbatim|lem:E3.2> (732); <verbatim|E-fixed-level.tex>,
  <verbatim|lem:E-fixed-level> (21), <verbatim|lem:E-stopping> (63);
  <verbatim|E-bounded-real.tex>, <verbatim|lem:E-coarse-bounded> (4),
  <verbatim|lem:E2-bounded-real> (64). Martin
  <verbatim|fixed-rung-convergence.tex>: graph contraction (10), fixed-input
  stability explicitly required (92), inserted functions (158), background
  density (183), positive normalized partition limit (287);
  <verbatim|fullmesh-correlation-cauchy.tex>: fixed-level data (31), higher
  modes (86), their omission (164), Cauchy argument (237), all distinct
  points (279); <verbatim|smooth-limits.tex>;
  <verbatim|selected-history-limits.tex> for the required actual stability
  input. Compare Trish <verbatim|E-fixed-level.tex> for warnings and stronger
  support bookkeeping.

  <strong|Checks.> The stopping estimate must have a prefactor uniform in the
  stopping level, or a demonstrated growth bound dominated by its decaying
  error. A subsequence obtained from Arzelà\UAscoli does not establish the
  full-sequence limit: prove Cauchy convergence first. A contraction bound
  for two graphs using the same map does not compare graphs using different
  cutoffs. Reflection positivity is passed only for admissible tests and
  finite identities; do not claim an unsubtracted distributional limit at
  collisions.

  <section|Original-literature checks for these
  chapters><label|original-literature-checks-for-these-chapters>

  The following are checked bibliographic starting points, not a completed
  theorem-equivalence audit. The author must read the cited theorem before
  substituting it for a source proof.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Kotecký\UPreiss, <em|Cluster expansion for abstract polymer
    models>, CMP <strong|103> (1986), 491\U498,
    <hlink|DOI|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01211762>. Publisher and original
    paper accessed through search on 2026-11-18. Use its abstract criterion
    after matching conventions; do not claim it proves this gauge model's
    activity bounds.

    <item>Baªaban, <em|Propagators and renormalization transformations for
    lattice gauge theories. I>, CMP <strong|95> (1984), 17\U40,
    <hlink|DOI|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01215753>. Publisher page
    identified. Relevant to U3's linear mechanism, not a replacement for the
    actual nonlinear/marked estimates.

    <item>Baªaban, <em|The variational problem and background fields in
    renormalization group method for lattice gauge theories>, CMP
    <strong|102> (1985), 277\U309, DOI 10.1007/BF01229381. Metadata located;
    precise theorem application remains to be checked against the original
    paper.

    <item>Brydges\UYau, <em|Grad \<varphi\> perturbations of massless
    Gaussian fields>, CMP <strong|129> (1990), 351\U392, DOI
    10.1007/BF02096987. Source bibliography and search metadata agree;
    inspect the original before invoking a norm or localization theorem.

    <item>The supplied Scott introduction gives useful original-work
    pointers: Baªaban 1987 ŸŸ4\U5 and pp. 252\U254; Baªaban 1989 large
    fields, Theorem 1; Gaw¦dzki\UKupiainen 1985 pp. 212, 218\U221;
    Brydges\UYau ŸŸ1\U2; Osterwalder\USeiler 1978;
    Fröhlich\UIsrael\ULieb\USimon 1978 Ÿ4. These exact-location claims are
    source leads, not independently verified citations in this blueprint.
  </itemize-minus>
</body>

